+### STM32 Nucleo USB FS Gateways
+
+The following application overlays enable all listed CAN channels, CAN FD, USB full speed
+on PA11/PA12, and a 32-bit TIM2 timestamp counter at 1 MHz. These are reference configurations
+for external CAN FD transceivers, not complete USB-to-CAN hardware designs. Channel numbers
+start at zero and follow FDCAN1, FDCAN2, FDCAN3. No GPIO-controlled termination is assumed.
+
+| Zephyr board | Channels | Flash / total SRAM (KiB) | Default application RAM (KiB) | CAN kernel (MHz) | CAN RX/TX pins, in channel order |
+| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | --- |
+| `nucleo_g0b1re` | 2 | 512 / 144 | 144 | 60 | PB8/PB9, PB12/PB13 |
+| `nucleo_g474re` | 3 | 512 / 128 | 128 | 80 | PB8/PB9, PB12/PB13, PA8/PA15 |
+| `nucleo_g491re` | 2 | 512 / 112 | 112 | 80 | PB8/PB9, PB12/PB13 |
+| `nucleo_g564re` (experimental) | 3 | 512 / 112 | 112 | 80 | PA6/PA7, PA4/PA5, PA8/PB7 |
+| `nucleo_h533re` | 2 | 512 / 272 | 128 | 80 | PB8/PB7, PB12/PB13 |
+| `nucleo_h563zi` | 2 | 2048 / 640 | 256 | 80 | PB8/PB9, PB12/PA10 |
+| `nucleo_c542rc` | 2 | 256 / 64 | 64 | 72 | PB8/PB9, PB12/PB13 |
+| `nucleo_c5a3zg` | 2 | 1024 / 256 | 256 | 80 | PB8/PB9, PB12/PB13 |
+| `nucleo_u3c5zi_q` | 2 | 2048 / 640 | 640 | 96 | PB8/PB9, PB5/PB13 |
+
+H5 configurations here use USB FS only. H533 SRAM banks are 128 + 80 + 64 KiB;
+H563 banks are 256 + 64 + 320 KiB. The extra banks are not automatically allocated to
+the application heap. G474's 128 KiB includes 32 KiB CCM; G491's 112 KiB includes
+16 KiB CCM, accessible through the SRAM alias used by Zephyr. These are not extra RAM.
+Dedicated CAN message RAM and USB packet memory are not application SRAM.
+
+The 60/80 MHz CAN clocks allow exact 1 Mbit/s nominal and 5 Mbit/s data timings.
+C542's 72 MHz and U3C5's 96 MHz allow 1/4 Mbit/s, but **not exact 5 Mbit/s** with
+these clock configurations. Bitrates remain host-selected; these overlays do not start
+CAN transmission. USB FS bandwidth is shared by all channels and does not guarantee
+simultaneous full-bus throughput. Runtime enumeration and CAN traffic still require board tests.
+
+I2C/SPI functions that conflict with the selected pins are disabled. H563 uses PA10 for
+FDCAN2 TX to leave the USB-C CC pin PB13 available. Connect to the target MCU's USB
+interface, not the ST-LINK USB interface; provide external USB wiring where the board
+does not expose target USB. The U3C5 Q package needs PB5 for FDCAN2 RX. Its overlay
+adds the missing upstream FDCAN2 node using the STM32U3C5 CMSIS register/IRQ definitions
+and HAL message-RAM layout. It targets the default TZEN=0 board, not the `/ns` variant.
+
+STM32G5 has an experimental out-of-tree port in `soc/roboparty/stm32g5x` and
+`boards/roboparty/nucleo_g564re`. It targets **STM32G564RE, TZEN=0, reset boot at
+0x08000000**, with no bootloader clock inheritance, power management or flash-writing
+driver. Other G5 SKUs are not yet build targets. The CPU stays at HSI 64 MHz, while PLL1
+Q provides 80 MHz to FDCAN (64 / 8 * 40 / 4); this does not exercise the 280 MHz CPU limit.
+USB uses HSI48 with CRS synchronization to host SOF. HSI-derived CAN clock accuracy still
+needs board testing. SRAM is 32 + 64 + 16 = 112 KiB and is contiguous in this memory map.
+
+The port uses unmodified [G5 device-pack](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32g5xx-dfp)
+and [HAL2 driver](https://github.com/STMicroelectronics/stm32g5xx-drivers) sources, pinned in
+`west.yml`. Register bases, IRQs, SRAM bank continuity, Flash size, RCC offsets and
+peripheral clock-enable masks are compile-time checked against the device pack. The fixed
+64 MHz system clock is also checked against the build configuration. GPIOA/B use AHB4
+enable bits 9/10 on G5. The pin assignments come from its LQFP64 pinout descriptor. The port
+reuses Zephyr's FDCAN, pinctrl, timer and USB FS drivers. G5 LL header wrappers, USB supply
+API aliases and the STM32 clock-header dispatch extension are generated only in the build
+directory; the installed Zephyr/HAL source trees are not modified. The custom RCC driver
+implements only this fixed gateway clock configuration and rejects unsupported boot clocks.
+
+Build one target with `west build -b nucleo_g474re cannectivity/app` in a configured
+workspace. To build the entire matrix and check the generated channel count, shared CAN
+clock configuration, USB FS and timestamp settings:
+
+```shell
+python cannectivity/scripts/stm32-fs-matrix.py \
+  --workspace /path/to/zephyr-workspace \
+  --build-root /path/to/test/tmp/cannectivity-build
+```
+
+Use Python 3.12+, CMake 3.28+, Zephyr SDK and Zephyr's base Python requirements (including
+PyYAML and pyelftools). The script selects the CMSIS 6, STM32 HAL and local CANnectivity
+modules explicitly; it writes per-board logs and `report.json` with ELF Flash/RAM usage,
+the chosen application RAM region, unallocated RAM and the ELF SHA-256. Unallocated RAM
+is linker headroom, not measured runtime free heap. For G5, it also checks CAN/USB pin
+availability, alternate functions and duplicate assignments against the device pack's
+LQFP64 descriptor, and records the pin map and descriptor SHA-256. This does not verify
+Nucleo solder bridges, external wiring or signal quality.
+`--board` selects a subset; `--inspect-only` checks existing outputs, which may predate
+source edits. It does not flash hardware. Run the offline pin-check regression tests with
+`python -m unittest discover -s cannectivity/tests/scripts`.
+`app.cannectivity.stm32_fs` also provides
+the nine-board Twister build matrix. For G5, run `west update stm32g5xx-dfp stm32g5xx-drivers`
+in a workspace using this manifest. In an existing workspace that does not import this
+manifest, obtain the two pinned repositories and pass `--g5-dfp /path/to/stm32g5xx-dfp`
+and `--g5-drivers /path/to/stm32g5xx-drivers` to the matrix script. Direct `west build`
+accepts the equivalent CMake options `-DSTM32G5_DFP_DIR=...` and
+`-DSTM32G5_DRIVERS_DIR=...` after `--`.
+
+Offline validation on 2026-11-12 used Zephyr `3a7ed4fcd55759ccd250bab65b0368bc8b5dc94b`,
+STM32 HAL `39130f29ae37c1db34095478ca02b6419b70dcdc` and Zephyr SDK 1.0.1.
+All nine builds and generated-configuration checks passed. Linked RAM usage was
+19,768-23,188 bytes; this includes statically reserved stacks/pools but is not a measured
+runtime stack high-water mark. No USB enumeration, CAN traffic or throughput test was run.
+
